      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 1: WHY TESTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="why">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Why There Are Tests at All</h2>

        <p>Two errors bracket this whole subject, and both are common enough to have
        wrecked real lives.</p>

        <p>The first says: <em>never examine yourself; look only to Christ.</em> It has
        a noble motive — it is trying to protect grace — but it cannot be squared with
        a New Testament that repeatedly tells believers to examine, test, prove, and
        confirm. The second says: <em>examine yourself constantly.</em> It also has a
        noble motive, and it produces people who take their spiritual pulse hourly and
        are never well, because a person who checks continuously will always find
        something wrong.</p>

        <p>The way through is to notice what the tests are <em>for</em>. John states it
        outright: he writes so that his readers <strong>may know</strong>. The tests
        are not a hurdle set in front of assurance. They are the instrument by which
        assurance is given.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Governing Distinction</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The tests are <strong>diagnostic, not
          meritorious.</strong> They tell you what is there. They do not create it, and
          failing one does not undo it.</p>
          <p>A thermometer does not make you well and it does not make you ill; it
          reports. Reading the New Testament's tests as though passing them earns
          standing turns the whole enterprise into the works-righteousness that Part I
          and Part II ruled out from Genesis 15 onward. Reading them as reports on a
          life is what John, Peter, Paul, and James are all doing.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 2: THREE TESTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="three">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Three Tests of 1 John</h2>

        <p>1 John is the New Testament's dedicated treatment of assurance, and it is
        built around three tests that the author cycles through three times rather than
        listing once. The repetition is deliberate: he keeps coming back because he
        does not want any one of them read alone.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Test</th><th>The question</th><th>Key texts</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Belief</strong><br><em>doctrinal</em></td>
              <td>Do you confess that Jesus is the Christ, come in the flesh, the Son of God?</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:22-23">2:22–23</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:2-3">4:2–3</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:1">5:1</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:5">5:5</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Love</strong><br><em>relational</em></td>
              <td>Do you love the brothers — actually, in deed, including the ones you did not choose?</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:9-11">2:9–11</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:14-18">3:14–18</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:7-8">4:7–8</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 4:20">4:20</a></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Obedience</strong><br><em>moral</em></td>
              <td>Do you keep his commandments — is the direction of your life toward the light?</td>
              <td><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:3-6">2:3–6</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:6-10">3:6–10</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 5:3">5:3</a></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Two features of how John uses them matter enormously.</p>

        <p><strong>First, he does not intend them as a scorecard.</strong> The same
        letter that says "no one born of God makes a practice of sinning"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:9">3:9</a>) says, in its opening paragraphs,
        that anyone claiming to be without sin is a liar
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 1:8">1:8</a>) and that when we do sin, we have
        an advocate (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 2:1">2:1</a>). John is not
        confused. He is describing a settled direction in 3:9 and an ongoing reality in
        1:8, and he expects his readers to hold both without panic.</p>

        <p><strong>Second, the love test is the one he presses hardest and the one
        modern readers weight least.</strong> He states it as bluntly as anything in
        the New Testament:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          We know that we have passed out of death into life, because we love the
          brothers. Whoever does not love abides in death.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:14">1 John 3:14</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Note the epistemology: <em>we know... because we love.</em> John treats love
        for fellow believers as a ground of knowledge about one's own state. And he
        immediately defines it against sentiment — "let us not love in word or talk but
        in deed and in truth" (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:18">3:18</a>) — with
        the concrete example of a brother in need and a believer with resources who
        closes his heart.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">A Test Worth Sitting With</span>
          Many people who are anxious about their salvation have never once considered
          the love test, because they have been trained to examine only their sins and
          their beliefs. It is often the most reassuring of the three, and the one most
          visible to others: <em>do you find yourself drawn to God's people? Does their
          company feel like home? Does their trouble move you to act?</em> A person who
          can answer yes to that has evidence that John regarded as decisive, whatever
          their internal weather.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 3: 2 COR 13:5
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="2cor">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">"Examine Yourselves" — What Paul Actually Meant</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Examine yourselves, to see whether you are in the faith. Test yourselves. Or
          do you not realise this about yourselves, that Jesus Christ is in you? —
          unless indeed you fail to meet the test!
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 13:5">2 Corinthians 13:5</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>This verse has probably generated more assurance anxiety than any other, and
        the anxiety comes from reading it apart from its paragraph.</p>

        <p>The Corinthians were demanding proof that Christ was speaking through Paul
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="2 Corinthians 13:3">13:3</a>). Paul's reply is a
        counter-punch, not a devotional exercise: <em>you want to test me? Test
        yourselves. You are my letter of recommendation. If Christ is in you — and he
        is — then my ministry among you is authenticated, because it is how you got
        here.</em> The Greek expects a positive answer to "do you not realise that
        Jesus Christ is in you?" and the qualifying clause at the end is a rhetorical
        edge, not the expected outcome. Paul goes on in the next verse to say he trusts
        they will find that <em>he</em> has not failed the test.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Reversal</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The New Testament's most famous
          self-examination text is an argument for the readers' assurance, deployed
          against people who were confident enough to be auditing an apostle.</p>
          <p>This does not make self-examination illegitimate — Scripture commands it
          elsewhere plainly. It does mean that 2 Corinthians 13:5 should not be the
          verse hung over a frightened believer's bed. Paul aimed it at the
          self-assured; using it on the self-doubting inverts its function.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 4: 2 PETER
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="peter">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">2 Peter's Ladder</h2>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Make every effort to supplement your faith with virtue, and virtue with
          knowledge, and knowledge with self-control, and self-control with
          steadfastness, and steadfastness with godliness, and godliness with brotherly
          affection, and brotherly affection with love... Therefore, brothers, be all
          the more diligent to confirm your calling and election, for if you practise
          these qualities you will never fall.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="2 Peter 1:5-10">2 Peter 1:5–10</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Three things are worth noticing, and the order of them is the point.</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The power comes first.</strong> Two verses earlier, Peter has
          said that God's divine power "has granted to us all things that pertain to
          life and godliness" (1:3) and made us partakers of the divine nature (1:4).
          The effort in verse 5 runs on supplied fuel. This is Philippians 2:12–13
          again, in a different hand.</li>
          <li><strong>"Confirm" means make evident, not make secure.</strong> Peter is
          not suggesting that God's election is unstable until we shore it up. He is
          saying these qualities are how a calling shows itself — to others, and to
          the one who has it.</li>
          <li><strong>The failure mode is described, and it is not anxiety.</strong>
          "Whoever lacks these qualities is so short-sighted that he is blind, having
          forgotten that he was cleansed from his former sins" (1:9). The person in
          danger, on Peter's account, is not the one worried about their state — it is
          the one who has forgotten what happened to them and stopped growing.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 5: FRUIT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="fruit">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Fruit — and Four Things It Is Not</h2>

        <p>"You will recognise them by their fruits"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 7:16">Matthew 7:16</a>) is the test Jesus
        gives, and the fruit is specified elsewhere:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          But the fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness,
          goodness, faithfulness, gentleness, self-control.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:22-23">Galatians 5:22–23</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>The list is worth reading slowly, because almost none of it is what
        religious communities usually measure. It is a list of qualities of character
        in relationship. Not one item is an achievement, a quantity, or a platform.</p>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">Four Things Mistaken for Fruit</span>
          <strong>Gifting is not fruit.</strong> Matthew 7:22's rejected professors
          prophesied, cast out demons, and did mighty works. Effectiveness in ministry
          is not on Paul's list and is explicitly insufficient on Jesus'.<br><br>
          <strong>Intensity is not fruit.</strong> Strong feeling in worship, deep
          conviction of sin, and emotional responsiveness are all good things and none
          of them appears in Galatians 5. The rocky-soil hearer in
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 4:16-17">Mark 4:16–17</a> receives the word
          "immediately with joy" and has no root.<br><br>
          <strong>Doctrinal precision is not fruit.</strong> It matters — the belief
          test is one of John's three — but a person can hold every article correctly
          and fail the other two tests entirely, which is roughly the complaint of
          <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 2:2-4">Revelation 2:2–4</a>.<br><br>
          <strong>Niceness is not fruit.</strong> Temperament is inherited; kindness is
          grown. A naturally placid person may show less real patience than an irascible
          one who has fought for it for thirty years. Fruit is measured against where a
          person started, which is why comparison with other Christians is worthless as
          a diagnostic.
        </div>

        <p>James makes the same point from the other end. His target is not faith but
        the claim to faith that never once shows up in behaviour:
        <em>"What good is it, my brothers, if someone says he has faith but does not
        have works? Can that faith save him?"</em>
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 2:14">James 2:14</a>). The question is
        rhetorical, and the word he uses at the end is not "faith" in general but
        <em>that</em> faith — the kind just described, which consists entirely of
        saying.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 6: NEGATIVE LISTS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="negative">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Negative Lists and the Word "Practice"</h2>

        <p>Several passages list behaviours and say that those who do them will not
        inherit the kingdom (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 6:9-10">1 Cor
        6:9–10</a>; <a class="ref" data-ref="Galatians 5:19-21">Gal 5:19–21</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Ephesians 5:5">Eph 5:5</a>;
        <a class="ref" data-ref="Revelation 21:8">Rev 21:8</a>). These are among the
        most weaponised texts in Christian history and among the most misread.</p>

        <p>The grammar carries the meaning. Paul's phrase in Galatians 5:21 is
        <em>hoi ta toiauta prassontes</em> — those who <em>practise</em> such things,
        a present participle of habitual, characteristic action. John's "no one born of
        God makes a practice of sinning"
        (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 John 3:9">1 John 3:9</a>) uses the same continuous
        construction. The distinction is between a person who <em>does</em> a sin and a
        person whose life <em>is</em> that sin — between falling and living there.</p>

        <p>And then there is the sentence that follows the harshest of these lists,
        which is quoted far less often than the list itself:</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          And such were some of you. But you were washed, you were sanctified, you were
          justified in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by the Spirit of our God.
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 6:11">1 Corinthians 6:11</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Paul lists the categories and then tells the Corinthians that some of them
        used to be in those categories and are not now — not because they cleaned
        themselves up before joining, but because they were washed. Any use of
        1 Corinthians 6:9–10 that stops at verse 10 has cut the paragraph in half at
        exactly the point where the gospel enters.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 7: HOW TO RUN THEM
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="howto">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Six Rules for Running the Tests</h2>

        <p>The tests are sound. Most of the damage done with them comes from method,
        not content.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 1</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Read direction, not position</div>
          <p>The question is not "where am I?" but "which way am I facing, over time?"
          A ship in a storm may be pointed briefly in every direction; the log book
          over a year tells you where it is going. Every biblical test is phrased in
          continuous terms — walking, abiding, practising, bearing — because they are
          measurements of a trajectory.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 2</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Read pattern, not incident</div>
          <p>One failure is data about a moment. A pattern is data about a life. David
          committed adultery and arranged a killing; Peter denied Christ three times
          with oaths. Neither incident, taken alone, would pass any inventory. Both men
          are treated by Scripture as belonging to God — and in both cases what
          followed the sin is what the text draws attention to.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 3</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Ask what your sin does to you</div>
          <p>This is the most useful single question in the whole examination, and it
          cuts cleanly. <strong>Does your sin grieve you, or have you made peace with
          it?</strong> Grief over sin is not a sign of being lost; it is a sign that
          something in you is at odds with it, and that something was not there
          before. The person in danger in the New Testament's warnings is not the one
          weeping over failure — it is the one who has stopped minding.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 4</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Do not run the tests in the dark, alone, at night</div>
          <p>Self-examination conducted in isolation, in a low mood, at 2 a.m., is not
          examination; it is rumination, and it reliably returns a false negative.
          Hebrews prescribes the opposite: <em>"exhort one another every day, as long
          as it is called 'today,' that none of you may be hardened"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Hebrews 3:13">Hebrews 3:13</a>). The New Testament
          assumes this work is done in company. Other people can see fruit in you that
          you cannot see in yourself — which is not flattery, it is a different and
          often better vantage point.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 5</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Do not confuse the test with the ground</div>
          <p>Evidence that you are in Christ is not the reason you are accepted. If the
          examination starts functioning as the basis of your standing rather than as a
          report on it, it has become the law, and it will condemn you — because it is
          supposed to. Run the tests, then go back and stand on the ground you stood on
          before you ran them.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Rule 6</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">Set a frequency, and keep it</div>
          <p>Scripture prescribes examination before the Lord's Supper
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="1 Corinthians 11:28">1 Cor 11:28</a>) and in
          seasons of drift; it never prescribes it hourly. A person who examines
          continuously is not being conscientious — they are performing an anxiety
          ritual, and the remedy is not a better examination but a scheduled one.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 8: TWO READERS
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="two">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Two Readers, Two Medicines</h2>

        <p>Part I noted that Ezekiel gave opposite-sounding words to opposite
        conditions — chapter 18 to people using doctrine to dodge responsibility,
        chapter 36 to people whose bones were dried up. That is the pastoral principle
        this section runs on, and it is the reason a study like this one is dangerous
        if read without self-location.</p>

        <table class="tg-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr><th></th><th>The Tender Conscience</th><th>The Hardened Heart</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>How they read this article</strong></td><td>Finds every warning and applies it to themselves</td><td>Finds every promise and applies it to themselves</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Their relation to sin</strong></td><td>Grieves over it, confesses it, fights it, fails, grieves again</td><td>Manages it, explains it, has stopped bringing it up</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>What they fear</strong></td><td>That they are not really saved</td><td>Very little</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Their prayer life</strong></td><td>Guilty and intermittent, but they keep returning</td><td>Formal, or lapsed without distress</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Response to correction</strong></td><td>Crushed, then repentant</td><td>Irritated, then dismissive</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>What Scripture says to them</strong></td><td>"A bruised reed he will not break" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Isaiah 42:3">Isa 42:3</a>); "neither do I condemn you" (<a class="ref" data-ref="John 8:11">Jn 8:11</a>); "he knows our frame" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Psalm 103:14">Ps 103:14</a>)</td><td>"Do you presume on the riches of his kindness... not knowing that God's kindness is meant to lead you to repentance?" (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 2:4">Rom 2:4</a>)</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>The medicine</strong></td><td>The gospel, applied directly and repeatedly; less introspection, not more</td><td>The warnings, read as addressed to them; the question of Luke 6:46</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Cruel Irony of This Subject</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">The people most likely to read an article on
          assurance carefully, worry about it, and apply its warnings to themselves are
          almost never the people the warnings are about.</p>
          <p>The New Testament's warnings are aimed at hardness, contempt, settled
          repudiation, and comfortable hypocrisy. They are not aimed at the anxious.
          If you have read this far because you are frightened, note carefully:
          <strong>fear about your standing is nowhere in Scripture listed as a mark of
          the lost.</strong> It is, quite often, the first sign of a conscience that
          has been made alive.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">And the Other Address, Made Plainly</span>
          If you recognised yourself in the right-hand column — if you can read
          Matthew 7:21–23 without discomfort, if your sin has become a matter of
          management rather than grief, if correction makes you angry rather than
          searching, if you have not spoken honestly to God in months and it has not
          troubled you — then the warnings in Part II are addressed to you, and no
          system in Part III offers you a way around them. Every tradition surveyed
          says the same thing to you, and it is not "you are fine." It is: <em>come
          back today, while it is still called today.</em> The door is open and you are
          not too far. That is Part V's subject, and it is written for you.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 9: FALSE ASSURANCE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="false">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">The Marks of False Assurance</h2>

        <p>Scripture does describe a confidence that is misplaced. Its marks are
        consistent and none of them is "feeling uncertain."</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Confession without obedience.</strong> <em>"Why do you call me
          'Lord, Lord,' and not do what I tell you?"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Luke 6:46">Luke 6:46</a>). The problem is not
          insufficient sincerity in the calling; it is the gap.</li>
          <li><strong>Profession contradicted by life.</strong> <em>"They profess to
          know God, but they deny him by their works"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Titus 1:16">Titus 1:16</a>).</li>
          <li><strong>Hearing without doing.</strong> <em>"Be doers of the word, and
          not hearers only, deceiving yourselves"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="James 1:22">James 1:22</a>) — note that the
          self-deception is located precisely in listening a lot.</li>
          <li><strong>Ministry as a substitute for relationship.</strong> Matthew
          7:22's résumé is impressive and the verdict is "I never knew you."</li>
          <li><strong>Grace treated as permission.</strong> <em>"Shall we continue in
          sin that grace may abound? By no means!"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Romans 6:1-2">Romans 6:1–2</a>). Paul's answer to
          the abuse of grace is never to add law; it is to say that the person asking
          has misunderstood what happened to them.</li>
          <li><strong>Comfort taken from a past event with no present life.</strong>
          Peter's "forgotten that he was cleansed" (2 Pet 1:9) is a description of
          someone whose evidence is entirely in the archive.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Read that list again and notice the pattern: every mark of false assurance
        is a form of <em>disconnection between claim and life</em>. Not one of them is
        a state of feeling. This is why the anxious believer reading the list finds
        nothing that fits, and why the person who ought to be searched by it typically
        reads past it without slowing down.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 10: DOUBT
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="doubt">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">Doubt Is Not Unbelief</h2>

        <p>Scripture's treatment of doubting believers is far gentler than the
        church's usually is, and the examples are not marginal figures.</p>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">John the Baptist</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">The man who identified the Lamb of God asked whether he had the right man</div>
          <p>From prison, John sends to ask: <em>"Are you the one who is to come, or
          shall we look for another?"</em>
          (<a class="ref" data-ref="Matthew 11:3">Matthew 11:3</a>). This is the
          prophet who saw the Spirit descend. Jesus does not rebuke him. He sends back
          evidence — the blind see, the lame walk, the poor have good news preached to
          them — and then turns to the crowd and says that among those born of women
          none has arisen greater than John. <strong>The doubt did not reduce the
          commendation.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">The father in Mark 9</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">"I believe; help my unbelief!"</div>
          <p>(<a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 9:24">Mark 9:24</a>.) A single sentence
          containing faith and its failure, offered honestly — and the boy is healed.
          The prayer of a divided heart is answered. Many Christians have never been
          told they are allowed to pray this.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-point">
          <span class="tg-point__label">Psalm 88</span>
          <div class="tg-point__heading">A psalm that ends in the dark</div>
          <p>Every other lament in the Psalter turns somewhere toward hope. Psalm 88
          does not; its last word is darkness. It is in the canon, it was sung in
          public worship, and God did not require it to end well before including it.
          A believer's worst season has scriptural precedent and a scriptural
          liturgy.</p>
        </div>

        <p>The useful distinction is between <strong>doubt</strong> — which is a
        condition of the mind, often accompanied by grief, and which keeps asking —
        and <strong>unbelief</strong> in the New Testament's sense, which is a settled
        turning away that has stopped asking. Doubt that prays is faith under pressure.
        Thomas said he would not believe without proof, and eight days later he was in
        the same room with the same disciples when Jesus came — <em>he was still
        there</em>, which turned out to matter more than his declaration.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 11: UNFORGIVABLE SIN
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="unforgivable">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">"Have I Committed the Unforgivable Sin?"</h2>

        <p>This question torments a small number of people severely, and it deserves a
        direct answer rather than a general reassurance.</p>

        <blockquote class="scripture">
          Truly, I say to you, all sins will be forgiven the children of man, and
          whatever blasphemies they utter, but whoever blasphemes against the Holy
          Spirit never has forgiveness, but is guilty of an eternal sin — for they were
          saying, "He has an unclean spirit."
          <cite><a class="ref" data-ref="Mark 3:28-30">Mark 3:28–30</a></cite>
        </blockquote>

        <p>Mark supplies the explanation himself in the last clause, and it is decisive
        for identifying the sin. The scribes had watched Jesus deliver a man and had
        concluded, in the face of the evidence, that the power doing it was demonic.
        The sin is the settled, clear-eyed attribution of the Holy Spirit's work to
        Satan by people who could see exactly what was happening. It is a condition of
        hardening so complete that the categories of good and evil have been inverted
        on purpose.</p>

        <div class="tg-principle">
          <span class="tg-principle__label">The Answer to the Question as It Is Actually Asked</span>
          <p class="tg-principle__text">A person capable of fearing that they have
          committed this sin has, by that fear, demonstrated that they have not.</p>
          <p>The sin is defined by hardness. Anguish about it is the opposite of
          hardness. Nobody who has genuinely inverted good and evil lies awake worrying
          about their standing before a God whose Spirit they have decided is a demon —
          they are not asking the question at all. The distress is the evidence. If
          this is your fear, it is worth reading that sentence twice, telling one
          mature Christian about it out loud, and then declining to relitigate it,
          because relitigating it is the mechanism by which it holds.</p>
        </div>

        <p>One further note, because it is often needed: intrusive blasphemous thoughts
        — unwanted, distressing, arriving unbidden and often at the worst moments — are
        a common feature of scrupulosity and of obsessive-compulsive presentations, and
        are not the sin of Mark 3. The distinguishing feature is that they are hated by
        the person having them. Where this is persistent and disabling, it is worth
        saying plainly that it is treatable, and that seeking help for it is not a
        failure of faith.</p>
      </section>

      <!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
           SECTION 12: USABLE
           ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

      <section class="tg-section" id="usable">
        <h2 class="tg-section__heading">A Usable Examination</h2>

        <p>Run this occasionally — not daily — and preferably having told someone you
        are doing it. Answer over a season of life, not a day.</p>

        <div class="tg-application">
          <span class="tg-application__label">Nine Questions</span>
          <p><strong>1.</strong> Do I confess Jesus as the Christ, the Son of God, come
          in the flesh, crucified and risen — and do I rest on him rather than on my
          record? <em>(1 John 4:2; 5:1)</em></p>
          <p><strong>2.</strong> Is there a direction to my life over the last several
          years that is toward God rather than away, however uneven? <em>(1 John 1:7)</em></p>
          <p><strong>3.</strong> When I sin, what happens next in me? <em>(2 Cor 7:10)</em></p>
          <p><strong>4.</strong> Do I love God's people — do I want to be with them,
          and do I act when they are in need? <em>(1 John 3:14–18)</em></p>
          <p><strong>5.</strong> Is there any sin I am currently defending, hiding, and
          intending to keep? <em>(1 John 1:6)</em></p>
          <p><strong>6.</strong> Do I want to obey — is God's command something I
          resent, or something I fail at and wish I did not? <em>(1 John 5:3;
          Rom 7:22)</em></p>
          <p><strong>7.</strong> Is there any fruit — patience, kindness, self-control
          — that is more present now than it was ten years ago? <em>(Gal 5:22–23)</em></p>
          <p><strong>8.</strong> Do I pray, however badly, and do I return after
          lapses? <em>(Rom 8:15)</em></p>
          <p><strong>9.</strong> If I were to die tonight, what would I say to God about
          why he should receive me? <em>(Phil 3:9)</em></p>
        </div>

        <div class="tg-note">
          <span class="tg-note__label">How to Read Your Answers</span>
          Question 9 is the only one with a wrong answer that is fatal on its own: any
          answer that begins with something you have done is the answer of a person who
          has not yet understood the gospel, however religious their life. Questions
          1–8 are evidence, and they are meant to be read <em>together</em> and
          <em>as a trend</em>. A "no" on any single one is a call to attention, not a
          verdict. A "no" on all of them, held over years, without distress, is the
          condition the New Testament's warnings are written about — and the response
          to that is not despair, it is Part V.
        </div>

        <p>Part V turns from examination to maintenance: how the fire goes out, what
        the first love is and how it is recovered, the ordinary means by which
        Christians are kept, and the way back for anyone who has been away.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="tg-reading">
        <h2 class="tg-reading__title">For Further Study</h2>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>The Letters of John</cite>, Colin Kruse (PNTC, 2000), or
          <cite>The Epistles of John</cite>, I. Howard Marshall (NICNT, 1978).
          <em>On how John's tests function and why 1:8 and 3:9 are not in
          contradiction.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Christian Assurance</cite>, and the classic <cite>A Treatise on
          Religious Affections</cite>, Jonathan Edwards (1746). <em>The most rigorous
          treatment ever written of distinguishing genuine from counterfeit religious
          experience. Also, honestly, a book that has damaged scrupulous readers —
          read it with Rule 5 in hand.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Spiritual Depression: Its Causes and Cure</cite>, D. Martyn
          Lloyd-Jones. Eerdmans, 1965. <em>Sermons on the believer who is not
          well. Still the best pastoral treatment of the tender conscience in
          print.</em>
        </p>
        <p class="tg-reading__item">
          <cite>Gentle and Lowly</cite>, Dane Ortlund. Crossway, 2020. <em>On
          Christ's disposition toward failing believers, drawn largely from Thomas
          Goodwin. Corrective reading for anyone whose examination keeps returning a
          false negative.</em>
        </p>
      </div>

